---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "ballerina farms protein powder",
      "ballerina farm protein powder",
      "ballerina farm protein review",
      "ballerina farm whey"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Clean Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-clean-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Ballerina Farm Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Ballerina Farm's Farmer Protein is a grass-fed whey blend with collagen, colostrum and hemp, sold at a premium. Here is what the 24 g on the bag is made of, what it costs per 25 g, and which flavor to try.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#the-brand">Who Makes Farmer Protein</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in the Bag</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#protein-blend">What the 24 g Is Made Of</a>
      <a href="#colostrum">Colostrum, Collagen and Fiber</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and the Prop 65 Note</a>
      <a href="#who-should-skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#vs-rivals">Ballerina Farm vs Clean-Label Rivals</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Ballerina Farm Farmer Protein is a carefully built, nicely sweetened whey blend with a clean ingredient list and a price that reflects the brand as much as the powder. Per the brand at the time of writing, each serving gives 24 g of protein from grass-fed whey, collagen peptides, bovine colostrum and hemp, with about 3 g of prebiotic fiber, and a bag of 25 to 30 servings costs about $63 to $70. That is roughly $2.20 to $2.90 per 25 g of protein, about twice a plain grass-fed whey.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ballerina Farm Farmer Protein, often searched as Ballerina Farms protein powder, is better than a celebrity product needs to be. The base is grass-fed whey, the sweeteners are stevia leaf and monk fruit, there are no artificial flavors, gums or fillers per the brand, and every flavor lists 24 g of protein per serving. Buyer reviews and press coverage consistently describe it as smooth and pleasant to drink, which is not true of every clean-label powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            The problem is value. At the brand's one-time price of about $70 a bag, or roughly $63 on subscription, and 25 to 30 servings per bag depending on flavor, you pay somewhere around $2.20 to $2.90 per 25 g of protein. A single-ingredient grass-fed whey usually costs about half that, or less. Some of the 24 g also comes from collagen, which is a weaker protein for muscle than whey, so the effective value is lower still.
          </p>

          <p>
            So who is it for? People who want a whey shake that tastes like a treat, with no sucralose and a short, recognizable label, and who are happy to pay for the colostrum, collagen and fiber extras. If that is you, Creamy Chocolate is the flavor to try first, and it is the one you can buy on Amazon. If cost per gram or maximum muscle-building protein is your priority, skip it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone weighing it against other short-label powders, our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">clean protein powder rankings</a> put Ballerina Farm next to single-ingredient and certified rivals. The short version: it is a good powder that is hard to justify on numbers alone, and easy to justify only if you value the extras and the taste.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A good-tasting, clean whey blend at a luxury price.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Buy Creamy Chocolate if you want a sucralose-free shake you will actually enjoy and the extras appeal to you. Buy a plain grass-fed whey if you mainly want protein per dollar.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="the-brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes Farmer Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ballerina Farm is the farm and food business run by Hannah Neeleman, a former ballerina who trained at Juilliard, and her family in Utah. The brand grew out of a working farm and a very large social media following into a food company that sells meat, dairy and baked goods, beef jerky, subscription boxes and, more recently, nutrition products under the Farmer name, including Farmer Protein and a hydration mix.
          </p>

          <p>
            That background shapes the product. The farm brand trades on whole-food, ranch-raised ideas, so the protein leans on grass-fed whey, colostrum and collagen rather than isolates and artificial sweeteners. It is a premium lifestyle product priced to match the brand, and it is sold mainly through Ballerina Farm's own site, with at least one flavor, Creamy Chocolate, also listed on Amazon at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fame cuts both ways in this category. A large audience means a product can sell well without being good, and it also means a bad product would be noticed quickly. In this case, independent write-ups and buyer reviews are broadly positive on taste and mixing, while dietitians quoted in press coverage have raised fair questions about the price and the unbranded share of each protein source. Those are the right questions, and we deal with both below.
          </p>

          <p>
            We have not tasted or tested Farmer Protein ourselves. This review is built from the brand's product page, retailer listings, nutrition-tracker entries for the label and published coverage. Where we report taste and mixing, it is buyer feedback, not our own impression. Where we could not confirm a figure, such as calories for every flavor, we say so instead of guessing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in the Bag
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every Farmer Protein flavor shares the same core, per the brand's product page at the time of writing: 100 percent grass-fed whey protein, bovine collagen peptides, calf-first bovine colostrum, hemp protein, inulin from chicory root, unrefined sea salt, stevia leaf and monk fruit. Flavor ingredients come on top, such as cocoa powder in Creamy Chocolate, vanilla extract and ground vanilla bean in Vanilla Bean, and maple sugar and cinnamon in Maple Cinnamon.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Flavor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories (trackers)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Flavor ingredients</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Creamy Chocolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 140</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cocoa powder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sold on Amazon</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vanilla Bean</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vanilla extract, vanilla bean</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most versatile</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Maple Cinnamon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Maple sugar, cinnamon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Contains added sugar</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peanut Butter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 170</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Peanut powder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Contains peanuts</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Calories vary more than protein. Nutrition trackers that copy the label list about 140 calories for Creamy Chocolate, about 120 for Vanilla Bean and about 170 for Peanut Butter, and one retailer lists a Creamy Chocolate scoop at about 37 g of powder. Treat those as approximate and check your bag, because the brand's own product page did not show a full nutrition panel for every flavor when we looked.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweetener system is a highlight. Stevia leaf and monk fruit replace sucralose and acesulfame potassium, which is why the powder appeals to people who avoid artificial sweeteners. Maple Cinnamon also contains maple sugar, so it is not the no-added-sugar choice. The inulin provides most of the roughly 3 g of fiber per scoop, which helps the creamy texture and adds a prebiotic fiber, but it can also cause gas in sensitive people.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergens follow the flavors. Every flavor contains milk. Peanut Butter adds peanuts, and Chocolate Coconut Almond adds almond and coconut. The brand states the powder is gluten-free, soy-free and egg-free, with no gums or fillers. That is a short, sensible label for a flavored protein, and much simpler than most mainstream tubs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Ballerina Farm Flavors and Alternatives, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ballerina Farm sells one protein powder, Farmer Protein, in a rotating set of flavors, including Creamy Chocolate, Vanilla Bean, Maple Cinnamon, Peanut Butter, Strawberry Creme and Chocolate Coconut Almond, plus seasonal flavors. The three below are the ones we would start with. Two clearly labelled alternatives from other brands follow for readers who want a grass-fed whey for much less per gram. Figures are per the brand or retailer listings at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="protein-blend" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What the 24 g Is Actually Made Of
          </h2>

          <p>
            The headline number is 24 g of protein, but it comes from four sources, and the label does not say how much each one contributes. Whey leads the ingredient list, so it is the largest share, but some part of the 24 g is collagen, some is colostrum and some is hemp. That matters because those proteins are not equal when it comes to building and repairing muscle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey is one of the best proteins available for muscle, rich in leucine and quickly digested. Colostrum proteins are dairy proteins too and are reasonably complete. Hemp is a complete plant protein, but less leucine-dense. Collagen is the weak link for muscle: it is low in leucine and missing tryptophan, so a gram of collagen does far less for muscle protein synthesis than a gram of whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, a scoop of Farmer Protein likely delivers a little less muscle-building punch than 24 g of pure whey, though how much less depends on the collagen share, which the brand does not publish. For most people having one shake a day alongside protein-rich meals, the difference is small. For someone counting every gram toward a lifting goal, it is a reason to prefer a plain whey or whey isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want to compare how different protein sources work, our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein guide</a> explains digestion and leucine, and our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">collagen protein guide</a> covers what collagen is good for. The fair reading of Farmer Protein is a whey shake with functional extras, not a stronger protein than whey because it has more ingredients.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="colostrum" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Colostrum, Collagen and Fiber: Worth Paying For?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Colostrum is the first milk a cow produces after giving birth, and it is rich in antibodies, growth factors and lactoferrin. Ballerina Farm uses calf-first colostrum, which the brand describes as collected after the calf has fed. Supplement companies market colostrum for gut and immune support, and some small studies have looked at outcomes such as gut permeability in athletes, but the evidence is limited and doses vary widely between studies.
          </p>

          <p>
            We cannot tell you how much colostrum is in a scoop, because the label does not separate it out. That makes it impossible to compare with the doses used in research. Treat the colostrum as a bonus ingredient that you may or may not notice, not as a reason in itself to pay more. If colostrum is the main thing you want, a standalone colostrum supplement with a stated dose is the more transparent way to buy it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen peptides are added for skin, hair and joint appeal. Research on collagen supplements has shown some promising but mixed results for skin and joint outcomes, usually at doses of around 2.5 to 15 g a day. Again, the label does not state the collagen dose in Farmer Protein, so you cannot know whether a scoop reaches those amounts.
          </p>

          <p>
            The roughly 3 g of fiber from chicory root inulin is the extra you can actually quantify. It is a prebiotic fiber that feeds gut bacteria and adds body to the shake. For most people that is a small plus. For people with irritable bowel syndrome or a low-FODMAP diet, inulin is a common trigger for gas and bloating, and it is the most likely reason a sensitive stomach would not get along with this powder.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-per-25g" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            The brand's own price at the time of writing was about $70 for a one-time bag, about $63 with the 10 percent subscription discount, and each bag holds 25 to 30 servings depending on flavor. At 24 g per serving, a bag holds roughly 600 to 720 g of protein. That puts the cost per 25 g at about $2.40 to $2.90 one-time and about $2.20 to $2.60 on subscription. Prices change, so treat these as approximate.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers (approximate)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">25-30</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Servings per bag</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~$63-70</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Price per bag</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2.20+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g of protein</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For comparison, a single-ingredient grass-fed whey concentrate such as Naked Whey typically lands somewhere around $1 to $1.50 per 25 g in its larger bags, and a premium grass-fed isolate such as Transparent Labs usually sits closer to $1.70 to $2. Ballerina Farm costs more than either, and roughly twice the plain concentrate, before accounting for the share of collagen in its 24 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Amazon listing for Creamy Chocolate is a 20-serving bag, so check the per-serving price rather than the bag price before you compare it with the brand's 25 to 30 serving bags. A variety pack on the brand site lets you try several flavors for less upfront, which is a sensible way to avoid committing $70 to a flavor you may not like.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">cheap protein powder guide</a> ranks powders purely on cost per 25 g, and it is a useful reality check before buying any premium tub. At one shake a day, Farmer Protein costs roughly $2.10 to $2.80 per day, or $65 to $85 a month, against roughly $30 to $45 for a plain grass-fed whey.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Testing and the Prop 65 Note
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ballerina Farm says Farmer Protein is third-party tested, with each new batch tested by a qualified third party, and press coverage reports that it is tested for heavy metals. That is the right practice for a dairy-and-hemp protein. We could not find a published certificate of analysis or a named testing lab on the pages we checked, so we cannot independently confirm the results.
          </p>

          <p>
            The product page also carries a California Proposition 65 warning that mentions lead. That sounds alarming, but context matters. Prop 65 requires a warning when a product could expose a consumer to a listed chemical above a very low safe-harbor level, and many cocoa, plant and protein products carry it. It is a signal to ask the brand for test data, not proof the product is unsafe. Cocoa-based flavors tend to carry more lead than vanilla ones across the whole category.
          </p>

          <p>
            Farmer Protein does not carry a sport certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, as far as we could confirm. Those programs test for banned substances and are the standard for drug-tested athletes. If you compete under testing, choose a certified powder instead and confirm the product in the certifier's database.
          </p>

          <p>
            If heavy metals or testing are your main concern, our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains how to read Prop 65 warnings and certificates of analysis. For most adults using one scoop a day, a whey-based powder from a brand that batch tests is a reasonable choice, and asking the brand for its latest lab report is free.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip Farmer Protein if price per gram matters to you. It is one of the most expensive whey-based powders you can buy, and a plain grass-fed whey or whey isolate gives you more muscle-building protein for a fraction of the cost. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder picks</a> are a better place to start if value is the priority.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are lactose intolerant or dairy-free. The brand lists grass-fed whey without stating on the page we checked whether it is concentrate or isolate, and colostrum is a dairy product, so lactose is present to some degree. People with a milk allergy must avoid it entirely. If you need a dairy-free shake, a pea or soy based powder is the right category.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it, or try a single flavor first, if you have irritable bowel syndrome or follow a low-FODMAP diet, because the chicory root inulin is a common trigger. Skip Peanut Butter and Chocolate Coconut Almond if you have nut or peanut allergies. And skip it if you are a drug-tested athlete, because there is no sport certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            Anyone who is pregnant or breastfeeding, has kidney disease, or takes medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a protein powder with colostrum and other functional extras. This review is not medical advice. Most healthy adults can use it without problems, but the extras make it a little less straightforward than a plain whey.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Ballerina Farm vs Clean-Label Rivals
          </h2>

          <p>
            Transparent Labs 100% Grass-Fed Whey Protein Isolate is the strongest alternative for most buyers. It lists about 28 g of protein per scoop depending on flavor, uses stevia rather than sucralose, and publishes lab results, at a noticeably lower cost per 25 g than Ballerina Farm. You lose the colostrum, collagen and fiber, but you gain more pure whey protein for less money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Naked Whey is the minimalist alternative: one ingredient, grass-fed whey concentrate, about 25 g of protein per serving per the label, at roughly half Ballerina Farm's cost per 25 g. It is unflavored and unsweetened, so you have to build the taste yourself in a smoothie. If you like knowing exactly what is in the scoop, it is the opposite approach to Ballerina Farm's blend.
          </p>

          <p>
            Among other short-label brands, our <a href="/just-ingredients-protein-powder-review/">Just Ingredients review</a> and <a href="/truvani-protein-powder-review/">Truvani review</a> cover two influencer-adjacent brands with similar clean-label pitches, and our <a href="/puori-protein-powder-review/">Puori review</a> looks at a grass-fed whey with published testing. Comparing them on cost per 25 g of protein is the quickest way to see what you are paying for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ballerina Farm's real advantage is the combination: grass-fed whey, natural sweeteners, fiber and functional extras in a powder that buyers say tastes good. No rival matches that exact mix. Whether that combination is worth roughly twice the price of plain grass-fed whey is a personal call, and our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">clean protein powder guide</a> is the place to compare the full field.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming 24 g means 24 g of whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 24 g comes from whey, collagen, colostrum and hemp, and the label does not break down each share. Collagen is low in leucine and missing tryptophan, so the muscle-building value of a scoop is a little lower than 24 g of pure whey. If muscle is the goal, compare it against a plain isolate.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing bag price, not cost per gram</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $70 bag of 25 to 30 servings and a $60 tub of 70 servings are very different deals. Divide the price by total grams of protein and multiply by 25. On that measure Ballerina Farm costs roughly twice as much as a plain grass-fed whey concentrate.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a full bag before trying a flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At about $70 a bag, a flavor you dislike is an expensive mistake. Use the brand's variety pack or Amazon's smaller 20-serving Creamy Chocolate bag to test taste and digestion first, then commit to a full bag or subscription once you know what works.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the inulin if you have a sensitive gut</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chicory root inulin provides most of the fiber, and it is a common trigger for gas and bloating in people with irritable bowel syndrome or on a low-FODMAP diet. If whey normally agrees with you but this powder does not, the inulin is the most likely reason.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the extras as proven benefits</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Colostrum and collagen have some promising but limited research, and the label does not state how much of either is in a scoop. Do not pay a premium expecting a specific gut, immune or skin result. Anyone pregnant, on medication or with kidney disease should ask a doctor first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ballerina Farm protein powder good?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is a well-made, clean-label whey blend that buyers say tastes good and mixes smoothly. It uses grass-fed whey, stevia and monk fruit, with no artificial flavors, gums or fillers per the brand. The weak point is value: at roughly $2.20 to $2.90 per 25 g of protein, it costs roughly twice a plain grass-fed whey.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Ballerina Farm Farmer Protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Each serving lists 24 g of protein, per the brand, from grass-fed whey, collagen peptides, bovine colostrum and hemp. Nutrition trackers that copy the label show about 120 to 170 calories depending on flavor, with Vanilla Bean the lightest and Peanut Butter the heaviest. Check your bag, because the brand updates flavors.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What sweetener does Ballerina Farm protein use?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Stevia leaf and monk fruit, per the brand's ingredient lists at the time of writing. There is no sucralose or acesulfame potassium. Maple Cinnamon also contains maple sugar, so it is the one flavor with an added sugar. The other flavors are marketed as having no added sugar.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ballerina Farm protein powder third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The brand says each new batch is tested by a qualified third party, and coverage reports heavy metal testing. We could not find a published certificate or named lab, and there is no sport certification such as NSF Certified for Sport that we could confirm. The product page also carries a Prop 65 lead warning, which is common for cocoa and protein products.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ballerina Farm protein powder good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can fit a weight loss diet, since a scoop gives 24 g of protein in about 120 to 170 calories with some fiber, which helps with fullness. But a plain whey isolate gives more protein for fewer calories and much less money. Our weight loss protein guide covers better-value options for people focused on calories.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ballerina Farm protein powder lactose-free?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. It is a whey-based powder with colostrum, both dairy ingredients, and every flavor is labelled as containing milk. The page we checked did not say whether the whey is concentrate or isolate. People with lactose intolerance may react, and anyone with a milk allergy should avoid it entirely and choose a pea or soy powder.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Where can I buy Ballerina Farm protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Mainly on Ballerina Farm's own website, where you can buy single bags, a subscription with about 10 percent off, or a variety pack. Creamy Chocolate is also listed on Amazon in a 20-serving bag at the time of writing. Compare the cost per serving across both, since bag sizes differ.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

